                <pb facs="0879" n="837" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p1/pg837"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 391</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>In testimony before the House Committee on Foreign Affairs on October 18,
                        Francis T. Williamson, Deputy
                    Director of the Office of Western European Affairs, stated the following:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“So in our policy with respect to Spain, we face then two problems. One is
                        the opposition of other European states to our policy in Spain; and second,
                        whether or not our policy will buttress or weaken the Franco Government, for which there is no
                        alternative solution at the present time. So we have to steer as middle a
                        course as possible between those two difficulties.”</p>
                </quote>
                <p>For the full text of Williamson’s
                    statement and the questions and answers which followed, see U.S. House of
                    Representatives, Committee on Foreign Affairs, <hi rendition="#italic">Selected
                        Executive Session Hearings of the Committee, 1951–56</hi>, <pb facs="0880"
                        n="838" xml:id="pg838"/> volume XV, <hi rendition="#italic">European
                        Problems</hi> (Washington, 1980), pages 123–127.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
